<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No module, and that is the whole design: no DDD_Module, which would declare one, and no
    [assembly: Module]. The boundary analyzer only polices assemblies that are a module, so every module
    may use what is declared here without either side publishing anything. Keep it that way by keeping it
    small: a type belongs here only when every module means exactly the same thing by it, and changing it
    is a change to all of them at once.

    Nothing generated here needs a name of its own either: Money is a multi-property value object, which
    Entity Framework stores inline as a complex type, so there is no converter method to call.

    DDDToolkit.EntityFramework and its generator are here for the same reason Ordering's contracts have
    them: the modules store Money, and the generator is what marks a value object [ComplexType] so
    Entity Framework stores it inline. It has to run in the assembly that declares the type.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit\DDDToolkit.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.Analyzers\DDDToolkit.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\Source\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework.Analyzers\DDDToolkit.EntityFramework.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
